    "body": "@annevk:\r\n> If you can invoke that method multiple times, do we need cancel() or should passing 0 or null be sufficient to clear it?\r\n\r\nWe'd still want the `close()` (née `cancel()`) because it's actually doing more than clearing the timeout. It's signaling internally to its `AbortSignal` that it won't ever trigger it from that point forward, and that the `AbortSignal` can clear it's set of abort algorithms. Anything downstream that might follow the `AbortSignal` would see `signal.closed = true` and know that they don't actually need to follow the signal since it won't be triggered.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
